  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Spectral Theory of Self-Adjoint Operators [S+N]\n\nThe big theorem: every bounded self-adjoint operator on a Hilbert space can be **diagonalised** in a generalised sense. For finite matrices this is the spectral theorem of linear algebra; for compact self-adjoint operators it's a discrete eigenbasis (previous notebook); for general bounded self-adjoint, the diagonalisation is via a **spectral measure**.\n\nThree layers, increasingly subtle:\n\n1. **Finite Hermitian matrices**: orthonormal basis of eigenvectors, real eigenvalues.\n2. **Compact self-adjoint operators**: countable orthonormal basis of eigenvectors, eigenvalues $\\to 0$.\n3. **Bounded self-adjoint operators**: spectrum may be a continuous interval; \"diagonalisation\" is integration against a spectral measure $E$.\n\nUnbounded self-adjoint operators add another layer (Stone's theorem, multiplication-operator model). We'll touch on it briefly at the end.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 28,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Spectral theorem in finite dimensions\n\nA Hermitian matrix $A \\in \\mathbb{C}^{n \\times n}$ ($A = A^*$) has $n$ real eigenvalues with an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors. Writing $A = U \\Lambda U^*$ with $U$ unitary and $\\Lambda$ real diagonal,\n\n$$A = \\sum_{k=1}^n \\lambda_k\\,P_k, \\qquad P_k = u_k u_k^*$$\n\nwhere $P_k$ is the projection onto the $k$-th eigenline.\n\nThis is the prototype. Maxima/numerics deliver it directly.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* A 4x4 Hermitian matrix */\nA_herm : ndarray(matrix(\n  [3.0, 1.0, 0.0, 0.0],\n  [1.0, 3.0, 1.0, 0.0],\n  [0.0, 1.0, 3.0, 1.0],\n  [0.0, 0.0, 1.0, 3.0]))$\n[evals, evecs] : np_eig(A_herm)$\nprint(\"Eigenvalues (all real, since A is Hermitian):\")$\nprint(np_to_list(evals))$\nprint()$\nprint(\"(Gershgorin: each eigenvalue lies within the off-diagonal row sum\")$\nprint(\" of a diagonal entry — here within 2 of 3, so σ(A) ⊂ [1, 5])\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 29,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Eigenvalues (all real, since A is Hermitian):\n[1.3819660112501055,2.3819660112501064,4.618033988749896,3.618033988749895]\n(Gershgorin: each eigenvalue lies within the off-diagonal row sum\n of a diagonal entry — here within 2 of 3, so σ(A) ⊂ [1, 5])"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Compact self-adjoint operators on Hilbert space\n\nFor compact self-adjoint $T$ on infinite-dimensional Hilbert space:\n\n$$T = \\sum_n \\lambda_n P_n, \\qquad \\lambda_n \\to 0,$$\n\nwhere $P_n$ is the orthogonal projection onto the eigenspace of $\\lambda_n$.\n\nSame structure as matrices, but only **countably many** non-zero eigenvalues, accumulating at 0 (which is always in the spectrum).\n\nA canonical example: the **integral operator on $L^2([0, 1])$** with symmetric kernel $K(x, y) = \\min(x, y) - x y$. The eigenfunctions are $\\sqrt{2} \\sin(n \\pi x)$ with eigenvalues $1/(n^2 \\pi^2)$.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Verify this kernel-eigenvalue claim by direct integration */\nn_test : 3$\nphi_n(x_, n) := sqrt(2) * sin(n * %pi * x_)$\nK_kernel(xv, yv) := min(xv, yv) - xv * yv$\n\n/* T φ_n = λ_n φ_n means ∫₀¹ K(x, y) φ_n(y) dy = λ_n φ_n(x).\n   Maxima won't integrate min(x, y) symbolically, so split the range at y = x:\n     y ≤ x :  K(x, y) = y - x y = y (1 - x)\n     y ≥ x :  K(x, y) = x - x y = x (1 - y)                           */\nx0 : 0.3$\nlo : integrate(y * (1 - x0) * phi_n(y, n_test), y, 0, x0)$\nhi : integrate(x0 * (1 - y) * phi_n(y, n_test), y, x0, 1)$\nresult_at : float(lo + hi)$\nexpected_lhs : (1.0 / (n_test^2 * float(%pi)^2)) * phi_n(x0, n_test)$\nprint(\"Tφ_3(0.3) =\", result_at)$\nprint(\"λ_3 · φ_3(0.3) =\", float(expected_lhs))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 30,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "rat: replaced 0.3 by 3/10 = 0.3\nrat: replaced 0.3 by 3/10 = 0.3\nrat: replaced 0.3 by 3/10 = 0.3\nrat: replaced 0.3 by 3/10 = 0.3\nrat: replaced 0.3 by 3/10 = 0.3\nrat: replaced 0.3 by 3/10 = 0.3\nrat: replaced 0.3 by 3/10 = 0.3\nrat: replaced 0.8999999999999999 by 9/10 = 0.9\nrat: replaced 0.8999999999999999 by 9/10 = 0.9\nrat: replaced -0.8999999999999999 by -9/10 = -0.9\nrat: replaced 0.7 by 7/10 = 0.7\nrat: replaced 0.3 by 3/10 = 0.3\nrat: replaced 0.7 by 7/10 = 0.7\nrat: replaced 0.7 by 7/10 = 0.7\nrat: replaced 0.3 by 3/10 = 0.3\nrat: replaced 0.7 by 7/10 = 0.7\nrat: replaced 0.8999999999999999 by 9/10 = 0.9\nrat: replaced 0.8999999999999999 by 9/10 = 0.9\nrat: replaced -0.8999999999999999 by -9/10 = -0.9\nTφ_3(0.3) = 0.004919886763091484\nλ_3 · φ_3(0.3) = 0.0049198867630914946"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Bounded self-adjoint — continuous spectrum\n\nFor non-compact self-adjoint operators, spectrum can be a continuous set. Example: the multiplication operator $M_x f(t) = t \\cdot f(t)$ on $L^2([0, 1])$. It's self-adjoint, but has *no eigenvalues at all*: $M_x f = \\lambda f$ would require $f(t)(t - \\lambda) = 0$ for all $t$, so $f = 0$ a.e.\n\nYet $M_x - \\lambda I$ is **not invertible** for $\\lambda \\in [0, 1]$: dividing by $(t - \\lambda)$ on the right doesn't produce something in $L^2$ near $t = \\lambda$.\n\nSo $\\sigma(M_x) = [0, 1]$ — entirely **continuous spectrum**, no eigenvalues.\n\nThe **spectral theorem** says: every bounded self-adjoint $T$ is *unitarily equivalent* to a multiplication operator on some $L^2$ space. So in the right basis, every self-adjoint $T$ acts by multiplication.\n\n## Functional calculus — applying functions to operators\n\nIf $T$ is bounded self-adjoint and $f : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{C}$ is continuous on $\\sigma(T)$, the operator $f(T)$ is well-defined via the spectral theorem:\n\n$$f(T) = \\int_{\\sigma(T)} f(\\lambda)\\,dE(\\lambda)$$\n\nwhere $E$ is the spectral measure of $T$. For a Hermitian matrix this is $f(A) = U f(\\Lambda) U^*$ with $f(\\Lambda)$ diagonal entries $f(\\lambda_k)$.\n\n**Examples** (on a self-adjoint $T$):\n- $T^2$ — square it: spectrum becomes $\\{\\lambda^2\\}$.\n- $e^{i T}$ — unitary, by spectral mapping.\n- $\\sqrt T$ for $T \\ge 0$ — unique positive square root.\n- $1/T$ for invertible $T$ — bounded inverse.\n\nThis is *the* tool for solving operator equations like $T u = f$ (resolvent), evolution equations $\\partial_t u = T u$ (semigroups, $u(t) = e^{tT} u(0)$), and many others.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Compute exp(A) for our 4x4 Hermitian via diagonalisation */\n[evals, U_evecs] : np_eig(A_herm)$\n/* Build diagonal exp(Λ) */\nexp_Lambda : np_zeros([4, 4])$\nfor i : 0 thru 3 do np_set(exp_Lambda, i, i, exp(np_ref(evals, i)))$\nexp_A_via_spectral : np_matmul(np_matmul(U_evecs, exp_Lambda),\n                               np_transpose(U_evecs))$\nexp_A_direct : np_expm(A_herm)$\nprint(\"exp(A) via spectral theorem (top-left 2x2):\")$\nprint(\"  [0,0] =\", np_ref(exp_A_via_spectral, 0, 0),\n      \"  vs np_expm =\", np_ref(exp_A_direct, 0, 0))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 31,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "exp(A) via spectral theorem (top-left 2x2):\n  [0,0] = 31.94825099268455   vs np_expm = 31.948250992684542"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Unbounded self-adjoint — Stone's theorem\n\nMost operators in physics (Hamiltonians, momentum, position) are **unbounded** self-adjoint. They aren't defined on the whole Hilbert space, but on a dense **domain**.\n\n**Stone's theorem.** If $T$ is unbounded self-adjoint, then $\\{e^{i t T}\\}_{t \\in \\mathbb{R}}$ is a strongly-continuous one-parameter unitary group on the Hilbert space. Conversely, every such group has a unique self-adjoint generator.\n\nThis is **the** machinery of quantum dynamics: Schrödinger's equation $i\\partial_t \\psi = H\\psi$ with self-adjoint Hamiltonian $H$ has the unique solution $\\psi(t) = e^{-i t H} \\psi(0)$.\n\n## Where Stage 5 leaves us\n\nFunctional analysis closes the abstract loop:\n\n- **Banach spaces** — completeness, infinite dimensions, equivalence-of-norms breaks.\n- **Hilbert spaces** — geometry returns: orthogonality, projections, Riesz, Fourier.\n- **Bounded operators** — the morphisms of the category.\n- **Hahn-Banach** — the dual is rich enough.\n- **Three big theorems** — uniform structure forced by Baire category.\n- **Compact operators** — discrete spectrum, the matrix-like case.\n- **Spectral theorem** — diagonalisation in disguise, even for continuous spectrum.\n\nThe whole apparatus underpins modern PDE theory, quantum mechanics, signal processing, ergodic theory, and probability theory. With Stage 5 done, you have the tools to read the foundational chapters of any of those subjects.\n\nThe final stage (manifolds + diff geom) takes us in a different direction: instead of generalising linear algebra, we generalise calculus to curved spaces.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Reference\n\nHall, B.C. (2013) *Quantum Theory for Mathematicians*. New York, NY: Springer New York. doi:[10.1007/978-1-4614-7116-5](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4614-7116-5) — chs. 7–10 (spectral theorem, bounded and unbounded; one-parameter unitary groups).\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}